{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And Moses heard it, and fell upon his face.</b> In tractate Sanhedrin (110a) they said: what report did he hear? It teaches that they suspected him regarding a married woman, as it says (Psalms 106:16): ״וַיְקַנְאוּ לְמֹשֶׁה בַּמַּחֲנֶה״ — each man was jealous of him on account of his wife. And this matter is very far from reason, and we do not learn from aggadic statements, for who can say that this suspicion was specifically at this time, since there is no allusion to it in the verse. Therefore I say that the author of this aggadah wished to explain the verse ״כִּי כָל הָעֵדָה כֻלָּם קְדֹשִׁים וּמַדּוּעַ תִּתְנַשְּׂאוּ עַל קְהַל ה׳״ — what does holiness have to do with haughtiness? And if you were to say that there is a relationship between them, and that holiness is worthy of preventing haughtiness, then he should have said ״וּמַדּוּעַ תִּתְנַשְּׂאוּ עַל קְהַל קֹדֶשׁ״ or ״עֵדָה קְדוֹשָׁה.״ Rather, surely this is what Korah said to Moses: for we find in tractate Sotah (4b) the Sages said: whoever is arrogant will in the end stumble with a married woman, and in another place they said (ibid.): whoever is arrogant is as though he transgresses all the forbidden relations, and it is known that in every place where you find holiness, there you find the boundary of forbidden relations, for the forbidden relations profane the holiness. And thus Korah said according to his own reasoning: behold, the whole congregation, all of them are holy and bounded from forbidden relations, and if so, why do you exalt yourselves to lord it over and to be arrogant, for this matter will cause you to stumble with a married woman and to profane the holiness of the people. Therefore he said ״עַל קְהַל ה׳,״ and did not say ״עַל קָהָל קָדוֹשׁ,״ for after the haughtiness there is no holiness here, for whoever is arrogant will in the end stumble with a married woman, and this matter causes profanation of the holiness of the people. And by this Moses understood that Korah suspected him that he would stumble with a married woman; therefore it says: ״And Moses heard it, and fell upon his face.״ And the reason for the matter appears to be that everyone who is arrogant wants himself to be supreme above all who are elevated; there is none besides him — he wishes to rule over the entire world to the exclusion of all else. And since every woman is subjugated under the hand of her husband, as it says (Genesis 3:16): ״וְהוּא יִמְשָׁל בָּךְ,״ therefore the arrogant one cannot tolerate even this dominion, that it should be in the hand of another. And therefore he specified specifically a married woman and not the other forbidden relations. And the language is precise, that he said 'in the end he will stumble with a married woman,' for everyone who is arrogant goes and scrutinizes every kind of dominion that he sees in another, to subjugate everything under his hand so that his hand may be over all dominions. And the man does not rest content with all the dominions he has already attained, until in the end he sets his eyes also on the dominion of a husband over his wife, and wishes to subjugate her under his hands — and this is the expression 'in the end,' for in truth this dominion which he seeks at the end is the end of every matter and the limit of them all. And this interpretation is very precious, both for this passage and for the language of the Gemara."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:41:36.669700310Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}